{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Arise, walk through the land.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the Holy One, blessed be He, desired that Abraham take hold of the Holy Land which He would give him and his seed from now on, and He said that he should walk through it lengthwise and breadthwise, like a man who gives his fellow a parcel of land as a gift and shows him its borders and tells him to take hold of it. By way of reason (sekhel): the term 'walking' is mentioned in connection with wisdom, in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 8:20): ״בהתהלכך תנחה אותך״, and it is written (Psalms 39:7): ״אך בצלם יתהלך איש״ — this is the form of the rational soul, and it is mentioned only among the singular righteous ones who seek wisdom, such as Noah and Enoch and Abraham and those like them. And since Abraham moved his intellect in search of wisdom from level to level, and moved the rational soul onward and journeying in the attainment of the all-inclusive, exalted matter, the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: ״קום התהלך בארץ״ — that is, set the form of your intellect in motion inquiring into the existents that are in the land. For we do not find that Abraham traversed the land lengthwise and breadthwise as the Holy One, blessed be He, told him; rather we find that he settled and did not walk — as it is written: ״ויאהל אברם ויבא וישב״. In any event, this walking is the movement of the rational soul and the stillness of the body, and this is ״ויאהל״ — from the language of ״יושב אהלים״ (dweller of tents), for the pursuit of wisdom requires movement of the rational soul and stillness of the body, the opposite of the needs of the body, which require movement of the body and stillness of the soul. And He said: ״כי לך אתננה אתן לך״ — I will give you knowledge and wisdom to know the essence of existents, in the manner of what is written (I Kings 5:26): ״ה' נתן חכמה לשלמה״:"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "Citation 